  "marks_lower_bound": 77,
  "marks_upper_bound": 246,
  "lower_bound_definition": "marks on the marker-bearing point only",
  "upper_bound_definition": "marks from the marker-bearing point to the end of its leaf — the detector's OWN sticky semantics (rows.py:318 in_alt_branch persists to the next question) had the marker matched",
  "papers_upper_bound_would_fully_reconcile": 11,
  "by_syllabus": {
   "0606": 17,
   "0625": 1
  }
 },
 "HONEST_VERDICT": "The defect is REAL and directly observed, but it is NOT the explanation for the overcount bucket. Even the upper bound leaves ~63% of the 673-mark bucket overcount unaccounted for, and 7 of the 18 affected papers are not reconciled even by the upper bound. It is also near-exclusively an Additional Mathematics phenomenon (17 of 18 affected papers are 0606), so it is a targeted fix, not a general one. Stated this way rather than reported as a solved bucket.",
 "SELECTION_EFFECT_WORTH_RECORDING": "Scanning the 250 SUCCESSFULLY-parsed schemes at /tmp/acc57-full/out found 2 candidate hits, BOTH false positives ('OR:' and a word beginning 'Or b'). That is not evidence the defect is rare — it is structural: a scheme exhibiting this defect overcounts and is escalated by mark_total_mismatch_escalating, so it CANNOT appear in the parsed corpus. Any prevalence for this defect measured over outputs/schemes/ or the parsed corpus is zero BY CONSTRUCTION. This is the narrowed-denominator shape (D18) in another costume, and it is why the measurement above deliberately runs over the FAILURE set instead.",
 "not_re_run": "Run 35's diagnosis, the #95 probe and #45's census were NOT re-run — all are on disk under BUILD/accuracy-runs/.",
